# Best Practice Policy Tree
~~~ For TPP [see Architectural Notes [1]]

## Fundamentals
	Master Administrators typically interact with Venafi Trust Protection Platform through the WebAdmin (VedAdmin) console, where the left pane displays the hierarchical folder structure known as the Policy Tree. Policies (or Policy Folders) are used to apply permissions, control workflow, lock and unlock attributes or features, and control settings related to Certificate Lifecycle Management. Best practice is for Master Administrators to carefully consider the Policy Tree Structure before creating or importing significant certificate inventory so as to standardize object configuration parameters and enforce security requirements. In the Venafi ecosystem, bad Policy Tree design is one of the top causes of permission creep, failed automated renewals, and unmanageable inheritance locks.
	
	**Locking and Unlocking attributes and settings**
	- Locked Attributes: Locks applied to attributes and settings on a policy (e.g., Organization name, approved domains, disabling key reuse) prevent users without the necessary access and control from overriding the locked attribute or setting in sub-policies they operate within.
	- Unlocked Attributes: Attributes and settings may be left unlocked (e.g., standard CA template or contact distribution list) to allow end users operating in low-level policies the ability to customize for specific team related purposes or edge cases.
	
	**Policies vs Objects**
	- For clarity, think of a Policy (or Policy Folder) as a folder on your computer that sets the security rules for everything stored inside it.
	- An Object is simply an individual file sitting inside that folder—like a specific certificate, server, or connection driver.
	- *Note for Master Admins*: Under the hood, TPP treats virtually everything (including policy folders themselves) as system objects, but in daily operations, "Object" refers to the resources inside the folder.
	
### The Policy Tree Structure
	To ensure future scalability and maintainability, any new or existing TPP deployment should maintain a well-structured Policy Tree. Typically, 3 levels constitute the minimum best practice Policy Tree structure; Top, Mid, and Low level. 
	
	**Top Level**  
	* This level is considered the highest (or lowest if you reverse perspective) and would define the core organization of sub-policies and objects. This is also the level where global values and permissions will be set. For instance, the Subject Information that your overall enterprise organization might use will be globally configured at this level so that those values inherit downward (or, again, if you reverse perspective - upward). 
	* *Subject DN & Baseline Governance*: Lock core attributes (Organization, Country, Approved SAN Domains) at the Top Tier so local team admins cannot generate non-compliant certificates or bypass corporate PKI standards.
	:Example: Top Level (Global)

	\VED\Policy
		├── Administration
		├── Certificate Authorities [see Architectural Notes [2]]
		├── Certificates
		├── Installations
		└── Venafi Operational Certificates

	**Mid Level**  
	* The Mid Level partitions the environment by Line of Business (LoB), functional team (SecOps, WebServices, ITOps), or network security zone. This is where primary operational permissions (RBAC), team contacts, default approvers, and Organizational Unit (OU) naming rules are applied.
	:Example: Mid Level (Line of Business / Organizational)
	
	\VED\Policy
		├── Administration
		│   ├── Credentials
		│   └── Workflows
		├── Certificate Authorities
		│   ├── DigiCert
		│   ├── Client_private_PKI
		│   └── Client_private_test_PKI
		├── Certificates
		│   ├── Discovered [see Architectural Notes [3]]
		│ 	│   ├── Default Vendor Issuers
		│ 	│   ├── Known Issuers
		│ 	│ 	│	├── DigiCert
		│ 	│ 	│	├── GlobalSign
		│ 	│ 	│	├── Client_private_PKI
		│ 	│ 	│	└── Symantec (legacy)
		│	│	├── Self-signed
		│	│	└── Unknown_other
		│	├── Security_Ops
		│	│	├── WAF_certs_auto
		│	│	├── Internal_certs
		│	│	└── External_certs
		│	├── WebServices
		│	│	├── CAPI_certs_auto
		│	│	├── Apache_certs_auto
		│	│	├── Nginx_certs_auto
		│	│	├── Internal_certs
		│	│	└── External_certs
		│	├── ITOps
		│	│	├── LB_certs_auto
		│	│	├── Internal_certs
		│	│	└── External_certs
		│	├── Network_zone1701
		│	│	├── Pike_certs_auto
		│	│	└── Kirk_certs_auto
		│	└── Network_zone1701c
		├── Installations
		│   ├── Agent [see Architectural Notes [4]]
		│   └── Agentless [see Architectural Notes [4]]
		│	    ├── Security_Ops
		│		│	├── WAF
		│		│	├── SecOps_IIS
		│		│	└── SecOps_AKV
		│	    ├── WebServices
		│		│	├── CAPI
		│		│	├── Apache
		│		│	└── Nginx
		│	    ├── ITOps
		│		│	├── LoadBalancers
		│		│	├── IoT
		│		│	└── ITOps_AKV
		│	    ├── Network_zone1701
		│	    ├── Network_zone1701c
		│		└── Network_zone1701d
		└── Venafi Operational Certificates
			├── {cert-object}
			├── {cert-object}
			└── {cert-object}

	**Low Level**  
	* The low level and any subsequent levels below this should be where various objects reside or where structure for very specific and infrequent use-cases are built.
	:Example: Low Level (Certificate Object / Use-Case Specific)
	
	\VED\Policy
		├── Administration
		│   ├── Credentials
		│	│   ├── {credential-object}
		│	│   ├── {credential-object}
		│	│   ├── {credential-object}
		│	│   ├── {credential-object}
		│	│	└── {credential-object}
		│	└── Workflows
		│	    ├── {workflow-object}
		│	    ├── {workflow-object}
		│	    ├── {workflow-object}
		│		└── {workflow-object}
		├── Certificate Authorities
		│	├── DigiCert
		│	│   ├── {DigiCert-ConnectorA}
