### Architectural Notes
	- [1] = Although this guide to the Policy Tree Structure and relevant Best Practices is specifically for the Trust Protection Platform (also known as Trust Protection Foundation or Machine Identity Security), many of the concepts here can and will map to TLSPC's Application Folder structure but not perfectly. For instance, TLSPC Application Folder structures have reduced breadth and specificity with regard to permissions, inheritance, and unique configuration tolerability. 
	- [2] = *Certificate Authorities* could exist at the Top Level or as a Mid Level policy within a Top Level policy such as the "Administration" policy. It depends on how the enterprise organization wishes to configure the CAs in TPP and what permissions they would like to grant. 
	- [3] = The *Discovered policy* and relevant sub-policies are crucial to *Network Discovery*, *CA Imports*, and other certificate discovery processes. 
	- [4] = Having an *Agent* and *Agentless* sub-policy in the Installations policy is a bit legacy. Nearly all enterprise organizations are performing "Agentless" certificate automations (colloquially known as Certificate Provisioning). If an enterprise organization does not know about or plan to use Venafi TPP Agents, then these two distinct policies are likely unnecessary and can be removed. 
	
